issue(id="0004" status="closed")
  title Model Pug code forms and control-flow constructs
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers formatter
    i-want code and control-flow syntax represented explicitly
    so-that the formatter can understand Pug programs instead of only nested markup-like lines
  premise.
    Pug includes significant non-markup syntax: buffered and unbuffered code,
    conditionals, loops, case statements, includes, inheritance, and mixins.
    Right now the formatter mostly preserves those lines as opaque statements,
    which works for idempotence but leaves no room for syntax-aware formatting
    or validation. Once statement heads and attributes are structured, the next
    major frontier is modeling the control-flow and code-bearing forms that make
    real Pug files more than HTML written with indentation.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Add explicit AST coverage for the major operator-prefixed code forms such as `-`, `=`, and `!=`.
    criterion Add syntax-aware handling for core block constructs such as `if`/`else`, `case`/`when`, `each`, and `while`.
    criterion Decide how includes, extends, blocks, and mixin declarations or calls fit into the same model or into adjacent statement categories.
    criterion Add tests that cover representative examples from the docs corpus without regressing lossless behavior on unsupported subforms.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") Keep `0004` focused on syntax modeling: plain `include`, `extends`, `block`, `mixin`, and mixin-call heads should land here, while file-resolution, inheritance-linking, and cross-file validation semantics stay out of scope for now.
    item(status="covered") Model `block` forms explicitly enough to separate the block target from the optional mode (`append` and `prepend`), but keep the target name itself as an opaque payload rather than trying to parse deeper block semantics in this task.
    item(status="covered") Treat mixin declarations and mixin calls as the same implementation slice rather than splitting them, because the shared argument and attribute syntax is one of the main remaining sources of raw fallback in the upstream corpus.
    item(status="covered") Keep `0004` permissive: once a head shape is recognized, structure it, but preserve malformed or still-unsupported subforms as raw or lossless fallback instead of introducing new formatter errors before task `0008`.
    item(status="covered") Keep JavaScript and argument payload parsing intentionally shallow for now; conditions, loop expressions, include paths or filters, block targets, mixin parameters, and mixin arguments should all remain opaque strings inside explicit head variants.
